1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,250
If you want to write something that's going to

2
00:00:02,250 --> 00:00:04,500
affect people emotionally, you have to do it emotionally.

3
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:06,460
And it has to cost you

4
00:00:06,460 --> 00:00:07,920
more than the time you're spending writing.

5
00:00:08,160 --> 00:00:11,200
It pushes me to my emotional and intellectual capabilities.

6
00:00:11,420 --> 00:00:15,990
I feel like when something is working, it is because all cylinders are firing

7
00:00:15,990 --> 00:00:20,560
and I am working at the very bleeding edge of what I am capable of.

8
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:23,800
Storytelling is a huge part of the human experience.

9
00:00:24,140 --> 00:00:25,840
But how do you tell a good story?

10
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:28,840
There's the cliché of a writer sitting at a desk

11
00:00:28,840 --> 00:00:31,840
wrestling with the page, trying to find their ever-evasive muse.

12
00:00:32,340 --> 00:00:34,840
There are elements of craft to consider, for sure.

13
00:00:35,340 --> 00:00:38,590
But for many authors, creativity comes from a place deep

14
00:00:38,590 --> 00:00:41,840
within themselves, and it looks different from writer to writer.

15
00:00:42,840 --> 00:00:46,840
So, what's the secret behind an unforgettable story?

16
00:00:52,840 --> 00:00:54,340
Welcome to Harvard Thinking, a podcast where

17
00:00:54,340 --> 00:00:55,840
the life of a writer is a mystery.

18
00:00:56,340 --> 00:00:58,340
Where the life of the mind meets everyday life.

19
00:00:59,340 --> 00:01:00,340
Today, I'm joined by…

20
00:01:00,840 --> 00:01:03,590
James Wood. I'm a professor of the practice

21
00:01:03,590 --> 00:01:06,340
of literary criticism at Harvard in the English department.

22
00:01:06,840 --> 00:01:09,840
He's also a literary critic for The New Yorker and

23
00:01:09,840 --> 00:01:12,840
the author of multiple books, including How Fiction Works. Then…

24
00:01:13,340 --> 00:01:16,340
Sam Marks. I am a senior lecturer in the English

25
00:01:16,340 --> 00:01:19,340
department in playwriting, and I also teach a TV writing class.

26
00:01:19,840 --> 00:01:21,840
For the past three years, he was

27
00:01:21,840 --> 00:01:23,840
also the director of creative writing. Then…

28
00:01:24,340 --> 00:01:25,340
Lauren Groff. I'm a novelist and writer.

29
00:01:25,840 --> 00:01:27,340
I'm a short story writer, and

30
00:01:27,340 --> 00:01:28,840
I own a bookstore in Gainesville, Florida.

31
00:01:29,340 --> 00:01:32,340
She was a 2018-2019 Radcliffe Fellow, and three of

32
00:01:32,340 --> 00:01:35,340
her novels have been finalists for the National Book Award.

33
00:01:35,840 --> 00:01:36,840
And our final guest…

34
00:01:37,340 --> 00:01:39,840
Nick White. I'm a short story writer and a novelist.

35
00:01:40,340 --> 00:01:41,840
He's also an associate senior

36
00:01:41,840 --> 00:01:43,340
lecturer on fiction at Harvard.

37
00:01:44,340 --> 00:01:46,340
And I'm Samantha Liney-Perfoss, your host

38
00:01:46,340 --> 00:01:48,340
and a writer for the Harvard Gazette.

39
00:01:49,340 --> 00:01:52,340
Today, we'll talk about storytelling with four writers, and

40
00:01:52,340 --> 00:01:55,340
how they take an idea and bring it to life.

41
00:01:56,340 --> 00:01:59,340
What is it that gets a story started for you?

42
00:01:59,340 --> 00:02:02,340
Is it an image or a character? Where do you begin?

43
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:04,590
It depends on the situation

44
00:02:04,590 --> 00:02:06,340
and the story that's being told.

45
00:02:06,840 --> 00:02:09,090
So, sometimes it'll be something that I've

46
00:02:09,090 --> 00:02:11,340
been thinking about for a very long time.

47
00:02:11,840 --> 00:02:15,090
It doesn't become a story until I experience something or

48
00:02:15,090 --> 00:02:18,340
I read something else that collides with the initial idea,

49
00:02:18,840 --> 00:02:21,590
that sort of blooms into a story

50
00:02:21,590 --> 00:02:24,340
with urgency and density and gravity and weight.

51
00:02:24,340 --> 00:02:27,090
And after that happens, it takes a

52
00:02:27,090 --> 00:02:29,840
much longer time to build in the subconscious.

53
00:02:30,340 --> 00:02:33,590
To me, I often am thinking about a thing, an idea or

54
00:02:33,590 --> 00:02:36,840
a character or a moment or an experience, like, for a long time.

55
00:02:37,340 --> 00:02:39,590
But I think that the story itself

56
00:02:39,590 --> 00:02:41,840
doesn't actually happen until I start writing it.

57
00:02:42,340 --> 00:02:43,840
That's when it actually unfolds.

58
00:02:44,340 --> 00:02:47,090
I found that generally, like, writing is not successful for

59
00:02:47,090 --> 00:02:49,840
me if I'm like, oh, and then this crazy twist happens.

60
00:02:50,340 --> 00:02:51,840
Like, that's usually a disaster.

61
00:02:51,840 --> 00:02:54,090
And so it's a combination of something I've been thinking about for

62
00:02:54,090 --> 00:02:56,340
a long time and also the immediate thread of whatever is going.

63
00:02:56,840 --> 00:02:59,840
It's in some ways like I'm not a musician, but like a

64
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:02,840
musician improvising, like you're searching for the right note or lick or whatever.

65
00:03:03,340 --> 00:03:05,090
And then you find the harmony and

66
00:03:05,090 --> 00:03:06,840
you kind of take it where it goes.

67
00:03:07,340 --> 00:03:08,840
I apologize to all the musicians out there who

68
00:03:08,840 --> 00:03:10,340
are like, this guy doesn't know what he's talking about.

69
00:03:10,840 --> 00:03:12,340
Yeah, I think I do a mix of both.

70
00:03:12,840 --> 00:03:17,090
There are still stories that I want to write that I think I've been thinking

71
00:03:17,090 --> 00:03:21,340
about for years that I just haven't figured out yet how to bring to the page.

72
00:03:21,840 --> 00:03:24,090
And then there are stories

73
00:03:24,090 --> 00:03:26,340
that come more easily to me.

74
00:03:26,840 --> 00:03:31,090
And then once I have what Anne Lamont calls that shitty first draft, I

75
00:03:31,090 --> 00:03:35,340
have something I can work with and shape once it's outside of my head.

76
00:03:35,840 --> 00:03:38,090
Because I think all my stories sound

77
00:03:38,090 --> 00:03:40,340
good to me when they're inside my head.

78
00:03:40,840 --> 00:03:44,090
And then when I put them in the cold, harsh light of the page

79
00:03:44,090 --> 00:03:47,340
and begin to go back and inspect it, I can see all its weaknesses.

80
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:49,590
But then I have something

81
00:03:49,590 --> 00:03:51,340
tangible that I can work with.

82
00:03:52,340 --> 00:04:00,340
I think speaking in terms of like images or characters, oftentimes for me, I find writing begins with a particular character

83
00:04:00,340 --> 00:04:08,340
and then just figuring out what that character wants and what is getting in the way of what that character wants.

84
00:04:08,840 --> 00:04:14,589
I like this question because if I think of beginnings, literally for me, knowing what a

85
00:04:14,589 --> 00:04:20,339
first line might be or knowing what an ending might be is very helpful to me.

86
00:04:20,339 --> 00:04:22,839
I have much less experience than

87
00:04:22,839 --> 00:04:25,340
the other panelists in actually writing stories.

88
00:04:25,840 --> 00:04:26,590
For instance, I've never written

89
00:04:26,590 --> 00:04:27,340
a short story in my life.

90
00:04:27,840 --> 00:04:29,340
I don't think I could.

91
00:04:29,840 --> 00:04:39,590
But if I expand slightly the definition of a narrative to be, say, a nonfiction account or even stretching it a little bit here, even a review, it can

92
00:04:39,590 --> 00:04:49,340
be very helpful to me sometimes, even if I don't know what's going to go in the middle of something, to know where I'm beginning and where I'm ending.

93
00:04:50,340 --> 00:04:51,840
Just knowing where I'm ending can do it.

94
00:04:52,840 --> 00:04:54,340
Nick, you mentioned characters.

95
00:04:54,840 --> 00:05:01,090
There's this idea out there about how you might think of a character, but then once they exist, they take on a

96
00:05:01,090 --> 00:05:07,340
life of their own and they start to do things that you maybe didn't think they were going to do when you started.

97
00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:09,340
Do any of you find that to be true?

98
00:05:09,840 --> 00:05:11,340
Or in what ways do you wrestle

99
00:05:11,340 --> 00:05:12,840
with the character development within your stories?

100
00:05:13,340 --> 00:05:14,340
I love when that happens.

101
00:05:14,840 --> 00:05:15,840
I'm working on a novel right now.

102
00:05:16,840 --> 00:05:19,840
I have like things in the draft broadly outlined.

103
00:05:20,340 --> 00:05:25,340
But the sort of scenes that I begin to write sometimes, especially when I have two or more characters

104
00:05:25,340 --> 00:05:30,340
in a scene, it can sometimes, especially in those first drafts, feel a little bit like a science experiment.

105
00:05:30,840 --> 00:05:35,590
So seeing how these characters are going to spark off of each other or putting the character in a

106
00:05:35,590 --> 00:05:40,340
particular situation, it can be really exciting to sort of go with it and have a kind of like looseness.

107
00:05:40,840 --> 00:05:43,590
I'm currently reading that book, Tony at Random,

108
00:05:43,590 --> 00:05:46,340
which is about Toni Morrison being an editor.

109
00:05:46,840 --> 00:05:48,090
And there's somewhere in the book

110
00:05:48,090 --> 00:05:49,340
where they talk about how she,

111
00:05:50,340 --> 00:05:53,340
thinks about characters when she's writing as whispering over

112
00:05:53,340 --> 00:05:56,340
her ear and telling her like, oh, that's just right.

113
00:05:56,840 --> 00:05:57,340
That's right.

114
00:05:57,840 --> 00:05:58,340
Oh, nope.

115
00:05:58,840 --> 00:05:59,340
That's not good enough.

116
00:05:59,840 --> 00:06:00,340
That's not good enough.

117
00:06:00,840 --> 00:06:02,340
I wish I could experience characters like that.

118
00:06:02,840 --> 00:06:04,340
I think that's a beautiful way of thinking about it.

119
00:06:04,840 --> 00:06:05,340
Yeah.

120
00:06:05,840 --> 00:06:06,840
A lot of times when I write

121
00:06:06,840 --> 00:06:07,840
plays, like the characters, I don't see them.

122
00:06:08,340 --> 00:06:08,840
I hear them.

123
00:06:09,340 --> 00:06:10,840
It's an oral, a U R A L form.

124
00:06:11,340 --> 00:06:14,840
And I often feel like they've come or they've appeared in my head or my imagination

125
00:06:14,840 --> 00:06:18,340
or whatever the play for a reason, but I didn't might not know what it is.

126
00:06:18,840 --> 00:06:19,340
And so it's, let's see.

127
00:06:19,840 --> 00:06:20,590
I think it's a good way to

128
00:06:20,590 --> 00:06:21,340
think about where we all go together.

129
00:06:21,840 --> 00:06:22,840
I think it also varies.

130
00:06:23,340 --> 00:06:25,090
Like sometimes characters are incredibly strong and they're very

131
00:06:25,090 --> 00:06:26,840
clear and you have a really good sense of them.

132
00:06:27,340 --> 00:06:30,090
And then sometimes they're more hazy and you have to spend more time

133
00:06:30,090 --> 00:06:32,840
with them and like chip away or see how they reveal themselves to you.

134
00:06:33,340 --> 00:06:38,090
I think often we think of character as a fixed idea

135
00:06:38,090 --> 00:06:42,840
that we have of specific people, but everyone is an animal.

136
00:06:43,340 --> 00:06:46,090
And so I think character can

137
00:06:46,090 --> 00:06:48,840
shift radically depending on the environment.

138
00:06:48,840 --> 00:06:51,090
And so I think character can have

139
00:06:51,090 --> 00:06:53,340
the same people having a breakup scene.

140
00:06:53,840 --> 00:06:56,340
But if you have it on a beach versus on

141
00:06:56,340 --> 00:06:58,840
the top of Mount Everest, it's going to go very differently.

142
00:06:59,340 --> 00:07:06,840
So paying attention to not only whatever that is the inflexible center of what your conception of this person is, but

143
00:07:06,840 --> 00:07:14,340
also paying attention to the true animal nature of being a person in the world is really important at the same time.

144
00:07:14,840 --> 00:07:16,340
Lauren, there was an interview that you did.

145
00:07:16,840 --> 00:07:17,590
And you were describing your

146
00:07:17,590 --> 00:07:18,340
creative process as a nuclear fusion.

147
00:07:19,340 --> 00:07:21,090
I was wondering if you could talk a little bit

148
00:07:21,090 --> 00:07:22,840
more about that and what exactly that means for you.

149
00:07:23,340 --> 00:07:25,090
Yeah, it's really like my

150
00:07:25,090 --> 00:07:26,840
literature is born out of literature.

151
00:07:27,340 --> 00:07:29,590
And I think it's possible to write

152
00:07:29,590 --> 00:07:31,840
a novel without ever having read a novel.

153
00:07:32,340 --> 00:07:34,590
But I think it's very vanishingly rare and

154
00:07:34,590 --> 00:07:36,840
it probably would be a very bad book.

155
00:07:37,340 --> 00:07:37,840
Just my opinion.

156
00:07:38,340 --> 00:07:39,840
James can tell us whether or not that's true.

157
00:07:40,340 --> 00:07:44,090
But I think if you have speak yourself

158
00:07:44,090 --> 00:07:47,840
in literature, in ideas, in other people's voices.

159
00:07:48,840 --> 00:07:52,340
And you're listening to the ghost of George Eliot

160
00:07:52,340 --> 00:07:55,840
and Charles Dickens and Toni Morrison and Langston Hughes.

161
00:07:56,340 --> 00:07:58,340
You're going through your day listening to these things.

162
00:07:58,840 --> 00:08:01,340
Of course, it's going to be nuclear, right?

163
00:08:01,840 --> 00:08:04,340
Because your ideas are always going to explode

164
00:08:04,340 --> 00:08:06,840
when they come in contact with other people.

165
00:08:07,340 --> 00:08:11,840
So I think that is one of the great and moving

166
00:08:11,840 --> 00:08:16,340
joys and mysteries and beauties of living this life of writing.

167
00:08:16,340 --> 00:08:18,840
You are constantly surrounded by

168
00:08:18,840 --> 00:08:21,340
explosive ideas of other people.

169
00:08:21,840 --> 00:08:23,340
Can I just go back to the character thing?

170
00:08:23,840 --> 00:08:26,590
Aristotle basically wrote the Poetics, which is like a doctrine

171
00:08:26,590 --> 00:08:29,340
on how to write plays in a very specific way.

172
00:08:29,840 --> 00:08:30,340
But they're very influential.

173
00:08:30,840 --> 00:08:33,339
Puts characters subservient to action, right?

174
00:08:33,839 --> 00:08:35,589
So that anything that the character does that

175
00:08:35,589 --> 00:08:37,339
doesn't feed the action to him is extraneous.

176
00:08:37,839 --> 00:08:40,339
I don't think that we live in that model anymore.

177
00:08:40,839 --> 00:08:43,089
But there's a sort of spectrum I think of as

178
00:08:43,089 --> 00:08:45,339
like character driven versus plot driven and how that goes.

179
00:08:46,340 --> 00:08:48,340
And I think sometimes it seems dishonest if

180
00:08:48,340 --> 00:08:50,340
a character is shoehorned into the plot, right?

181
00:08:50,840 --> 00:08:52,840
But then also sometimes there's the experience of

182
00:08:52,840 --> 00:08:54,840
listening to characters where you're like, I don't care.

183
00:08:55,340 --> 00:08:55,840
What's the story?

184
00:08:56,340 --> 00:08:57,340
So I think it's like

185
00:08:57,340 --> 00:08:58,340
a, I think it's a balance.

186
00:08:58,840 --> 00:09:00,590
But Sam, the thing about

187
00:09:00,590 --> 00:09:02,340
the action and character breaking apart.

188
00:09:02,840 --> 00:09:06,090
Actually, if you think of a great deal of the

189
00:09:06,090 --> 00:09:09,340
kind of TV writing that I'm sure you would never do,

190
00:09:09,840 --> 00:09:12,840
but that is just the stuff that's out there

191
00:09:12,840 --> 00:09:15,840
now, an enormous amount of it is just simply,

192
00:09:15,840 --> 00:09:17,340
plotting for the sake of plotting.

193
00:09:17,840 --> 00:09:18,340
Yeah.

194
00:09:18,840 --> 00:09:22,090
With ridiculous twists and turns that even within the confines

195
00:09:22,090 --> 00:09:25,340
of the thriller genre don't really make any sense at all.

196
00:09:25,840 --> 00:09:29,090
And you'll sometimes encounter a piece by a student

197
00:09:29,090 --> 00:09:32,340
and it'll be trundling along and then suddenly in effect,

198
00:09:32,840 --> 00:09:35,090
they get into a spaceship and go off to Mars and

199
00:09:35,090 --> 00:09:37,340
you say, now, why did you do that thing on page eight?

200
00:09:37,840 --> 00:09:38,840
And they'll just say, I don't know.

201
00:09:39,340 --> 00:09:41,340
I just, because I can, I just felt like it.

202
00:09:41,840 --> 00:09:43,340
So this is a bit of a problem.

203
00:09:43,840 --> 00:09:45,340
I fully agree with that.

204
00:09:45,840 --> 00:09:49,840
I think that so many students and so many writers in general, like

205
00:09:49,840 --> 00:09:53,840
sometimes I think about writing as a process of confronting how you confront challenge.

206
00:09:54,340 --> 00:09:56,340
And I think often it's like, I want to quit.

207
00:09:56,840 --> 00:09:57,840
I want to take a break.

208
00:09:58,340 --> 00:09:59,340
I want to have a drink or whatever.

209
00:09:59,840 --> 00:10:01,340
I want to have a spaceship, right?

210
00:10:01,840 --> 00:10:04,840
I want someone to rescue me from the process of having to figure out

211
00:10:04,840 --> 00:10:07,840
what the heck I have to do because to figure it out is incredibly painstaking.

212
00:10:08,340 --> 00:10:08,840
Right.

213
00:10:09,340 --> 00:10:11,840
And that's the like cliche of the writer, like tearing their hair

214
00:10:11,840 --> 00:10:14,340
out or whatever, because it is hard to figure out what happens next.

215
00:10:14,340 --> 00:10:17,840
I'm now thinking of this story that ends with a character going

216
00:10:17,840 --> 00:10:21,340
off in a spaceship and what my response to the student would be.

217
00:10:21,840 --> 00:10:23,090
The story begins when they

218
00:10:23,090 --> 00:10:24,340
enter the spaceship and go off.

219
00:10:24,840 --> 00:10:26,840
Why are they, that's the beginning.

220
00:10:27,340 --> 00:10:31,340
I also liked what you said about a lot of times how we deal with conflict

221
00:10:31,340 --> 00:10:35,340
in our everyday lives is like related to how we do that in our writerly sense.

222
00:10:35,840 --> 00:10:39,340
I can feel that, but I also feel that there are ways in which I

223
00:10:39,340 --> 00:10:42,840
allow my characters to be much braver on the page than I am and much bolder.

224
00:10:42,840 --> 00:10:45,840
And I think that is a really great

225
00:10:45,840 --> 00:10:48,840
way to think about fiction and inhabiting characters.

226
00:10:49,340 --> 00:10:50,590
Have your characters do the

227
00:10:50,590 --> 00:10:51,840
thing that you're afraid to do.

228
00:10:52,340 --> 00:10:55,840
I think that is a really interesting prompt.

229
00:10:56,340 --> 00:10:58,590
That actually makes me think like we've talked

230
00:10:58,590 --> 00:11:00,840
about character driven, we've talked about plot driven.

231
00:11:01,340 --> 00:11:03,090
I'm also curious how much of it

232
00:11:03,090 --> 00:11:04,840
is like soul of the writer driven.

233
00:11:05,340 --> 00:11:07,090
How much of yourselves do you feel

234
00:11:07,090 --> 00:11:08,840
like you are putting into these stories?

235
00:11:09,340 --> 00:11:10,590
I think for me, my

236
00:11:10,590 --> 00:11:11,840
first novel was very much author-driven.

237
00:11:11,840 --> 00:11:14,340
It wasn't very much autobiographical, but it was

238
00:11:14,340 --> 00:11:16,840
also kind of like an alternative history for myself.

239
00:11:17,340 --> 00:11:21,090
It dealt a lot with conversion therapy and being gay

240
00:11:21,090 --> 00:11:24,840
from Mississippi and having a family that would not be accepting.

241
00:11:25,340 --> 00:11:29,090
And dealing with all of that mess and trauma on the page

242
00:11:29,090 --> 00:11:32,840
in a way that I felt unable to do in my real life.

243
00:11:33,340 --> 00:11:36,590
But I also have been thinking a lot about this because

244
00:11:36,590 --> 00:11:39,840
I've been talking to my students a lot about setting and place.

245
00:11:39,840 --> 00:11:44,340
And I find it really ironic that I spent

246
00:11:44,340 --> 00:11:48,840
so much of my time growing up in Mississippi.

247
00:11:49,340 --> 00:11:51,340
I'm from a really small town called Possum Neck.

248
00:11:51,840 --> 00:11:53,840
