And I get two chapters in and

510
00:22:27,340 --> 00:22:29,340
I'm like, it's just not for me.

511
00:22:29,840 --> 00:22:31,840
So as a professional critic, I'm

512
00:22:31,840 --> 00:22:33,840
wondering your take on that question.

513
00:22:34,340 --> 00:22:40,840
I would say, and I bet I'm joined here by everyone else, that I'm responding to and looking

514
00:22:40,840 --> 00:22:47,340
for a kind of vitality, a sort of just a sort of liveliness and life on the page.

515
00:22:47,840 --> 00:22:50,840
And in that sense, once you've got that,

516
00:22:50,840 --> 00:22:53,840
other questions like, is this a realist novel?

517
00:22:53,840 --> 00:22:55,340
Is this a postmodern experimental novel?

518
00:22:55,840 --> 00:22:57,340
Is it broken into numbered paragraphs?

519
00:22:57,840 --> 00:22:58,340
All that stuff.

520
00:22:58,840 --> 00:23:00,340
That's not to the point, is it?

521
00:23:00,840 --> 00:23:04,090
The thing that we're trying to feel and find

522
00:23:04,090 --> 00:23:07,340
is just something very alive that draws you in.

523
00:23:07,840 --> 00:23:13,590
I have a very strong memory of back in about 2011, standing in

524
00:23:13,590 --> 00:23:19,340
the kitchen at the kitchen counter and opening a package from Archipelago Books.

525
00:23:19,840 --> 00:23:21,590
And they had sent me just on

526
00:23:21,590 --> 00:23:23,340
spec the first volume of the book.

527
00:23:23,840 --> 00:23:26,340
And that's a cover of Canaris Guards, My Struggle.

528
00:23:26,840 --> 00:23:28,340
And I didn't know anything about it.

529
00:23:28,840 --> 00:23:29,840
I'd never heard of him.

530
00:23:30,340 --> 00:23:31,340
I knew nothing about it.

531
00:23:31,840 --> 00:23:33,090
And it was just like a little note saying,

532
00:23:33,090 --> 00:23:34,340
this seems like you might be interested in this.

533
00:23:34,840 --> 00:23:37,080
So I opened it and I just started reading it.

534
00:23:37,840 --> 00:23:39,840
And I was standing there for three or four pages.

535
00:23:40,840 --> 00:23:42,840
And I thought, this has something, this has it.

536
00:23:43,840 --> 00:23:45,340
It's drawing me in, it has real vitality.

537
00:23:45,840 --> 00:23:47,340
So above all, I'm just looking

538
00:23:47,340 --> 00:23:48,840
for that thing, the non deadness.

539
00:23:49,840 --> 00:23:50,590
When I pick up a book

540
00:23:50,590 --> 00:23:51,340
to read it, I am always,

541
00:23:51,340 --> 00:23:53,780
Always, I think, in the mindset where

542
00:23:53,780 --> 00:23:56,220
I am reading with my arms uncrossed.

543
00:23:56,260 --> 00:23:57,560
Like, I want to be impressed.

544
00:23:57,679 --> 00:23:58,939
I want to be taken somewhere.

545
00:23:59,139 --> 00:24:00,760
I want to sort of be drawn in.

546
00:24:00,760 --> 00:24:04,280
And I never underestimate a book or a

547
00:24:04,280 --> 00:24:07,800
story's power to just make me forget everything else.

548
00:24:08,520 --> 00:24:12,079
Thinking about that vitality or that life, I feel like that might be

549
00:24:12,079 --> 00:24:15,639
a sign that a story is working, either for you or just in general.

550
00:24:15,640 --> 00:24:18,570
As you are writing your own stories, how

551
00:24:18,570 --> 00:24:21,500
do you know whether or not it's working?

552
00:24:22,060 --> 00:24:24,660
If it comes close, you can feel it.

553
00:24:24,700 --> 00:24:26,480
It's almost like a vibrational thing.

554
00:24:26,480 --> 00:24:27,820
You're writing into it.

555
00:24:27,920 --> 00:24:29,760
It feels good, right?

556
00:24:29,800 --> 00:24:34,270
Even if it feels bad to write it, it feels good because you're coming

557
00:24:34,270 --> 00:24:38,740
as close as possible to this thing that you want so badly to do.

558
00:24:39,260 --> 00:24:41,700
And that doesn't mean that everyone who's going to read it is

559
00:24:41,700 --> 00:24:44,140
going to like it, right, or going to feel the same vibration.

560
00:24:44,620 --> 00:24:45,600
But you feel it.

561
00:24:45,660 --> 00:24:46,620
And you sense it.

562
00:24:46,680 --> 00:24:47,940
And you know that it's there.

563
00:24:48,240 --> 00:24:50,390
And it takes a very long time as a writer

564
00:24:50,390 --> 00:24:52,540
to actually develop a sense of that in your own work.

565
00:24:53,120 --> 00:24:55,890
I just really appreciate, Lauren, how much you talk about how

566
00:24:55,890 --> 00:24:58,660
things feel at the vibrations and the physical nature of it.

567
00:24:58,880 --> 00:25:01,940
I feel like you're talking about success at Harvard.

568
00:25:02,060 --> 00:25:03,670
And I think that a thing at Harvard,

569
00:25:03,670 --> 00:25:05,280
you're right, most of the students are very successful.

570
00:25:05,700 --> 00:25:08,290
And most of the success has come at an intellectual level

571
00:25:08,290 --> 00:25:10,880
as they plan their plays or their things in some ways.

572
00:25:11,400 --> 00:25:13,290
But I think just returning to feeling

573
00:25:13,290 --> 00:25:15,180
or vibrations, I just find it really useful.

574
00:25:15,360 --> 00:25:15,620
And I think I've learned a lot.

575
00:25:15,620 --> 00:25:16,719
I think I might use it in my class.

576
00:25:16,879 --> 00:25:17,280
That's all.

577
00:25:17,659 --> 00:25:18,199
And I'll credit you.

578
00:25:18,300 --> 00:25:18,719
I'll credit you.

579
00:25:19,219 --> 00:25:19,439
Good.

580
00:25:19,659 --> 00:25:22,479
Sometimes we do have to detach our intellect from the thing

581
00:25:22,479 --> 00:25:25,300
that we're trying to do because it's not all about intellect.

582
00:25:25,500 --> 00:25:28,739
Eventually, in the editing process, that's when we apply it.

583
00:25:29,000 --> 00:25:31,030
If you want to write something that's going to

584
00:25:31,030 --> 00:25:33,060
affect people emotionally, you have to do it emotionally.

585
00:25:33,600 --> 00:25:35,029
And it has to cost you

586
00:25:35,029 --> 00:25:36,459
more than the time you're spending writing.

587
00:25:36,620 --> 00:25:39,739
It pushes me to my emotional and intellectual capabilities.

588
00:25:39,739 --> 00:25:42,309
I feel like when something is working,

589
00:25:42,309 --> 00:25:44,879
it is because all cylinders are firing.

590
00:25:44,880 --> 00:25:46,960
And I am working at the very

591
00:25:46,960 --> 00:25:49,040
bleeding edge of what I am capable of.

592
00:25:49,300 --> 00:25:51,290
I'd also say, just as a tip, I don't

593
00:25:51,290 --> 00:25:53,280
know if it's useful for any of our listeners.

594
00:25:53,580 --> 00:25:57,080
First of all, I tend to just quietly read under

595
00:25:57,080 --> 00:26:00,580
my breath as I'm reading through a paragraph or two.

596
00:26:00,840 --> 00:26:02,440
I just, I quietly read it

597
00:26:02,440 --> 00:26:04,040
so that I can get a sense.

598
00:26:04,400 --> 00:26:07,100
But I also think, actually, just a more vocal

599
00:26:07,100 --> 00:26:09,800
version of that, of actually reading something out aloud.

600
00:26:09,860 --> 00:26:10,720
It's interesting, isn't it?

601
00:26:10,720 --> 00:26:12,720
When you read from your work in a

602
00:26:12,720 --> 00:26:14,720
bookshop, I don't know if others have had this.

603
00:26:14,720 --> 00:26:15,640
I've certainly had it.

604
00:26:16,140 --> 00:26:18,820
You agree to read a few pages, and

605
00:26:18,820 --> 00:26:21,500
suddenly you find you've edited out a sentence.

606
00:26:22,140 --> 00:26:25,320
It's like some weird kind of bullshit detector went off, and

607
00:26:25,320 --> 00:26:28,500
you thought, ah, I can't pull that one off on the room.

608
00:26:28,920 --> 00:26:31,160
And there's probably a good reason for that, right?

609
00:26:31,180 --> 00:26:33,150
And it should have been

610
00:26:33,150 --> 00:26:35,120
left out in the original book.

611
00:26:35,300 --> 00:26:37,860
So I just think that's the estranging thing.

612
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:40,420
Always it's a kind of, you want estrangement, don't you?

613
00:26:40,420 --> 00:26:42,480
Because you're trying to split yourself into two.

614
00:26:42,880 --> 00:26:44,420
This other person, this colder.

615
00:26:44,720 --> 00:26:47,020
This person who is ready to murder

616
00:26:47,020 --> 00:26:49,320
the darlings will ideally read what you've written.

617
00:26:49,480 --> 00:26:50,760
And that's very difficult to do.

618
00:26:51,480 --> 00:26:53,040
Reading aloud is so important.

619
00:26:53,540 --> 00:26:58,620
I work in my office here at Lamont, and I'm sure that when I was back at OSU as

620
00:26:58,620 --> 00:27:03,700
a professor there, my colleagues who were nearby probably thought I was insane because I'm reading my stuff aloud.

621
00:27:03,700 --> 00:27:07,400
And it must appear that I am, in some ways, having a

622
00:27:07,400 --> 00:27:11,100
breakdown, especially depending on what part of the story or chapter I'm reading.

623
00:27:11,180 --> 00:27:11,980
But that is so true.

624
00:27:12,480 --> 00:27:14,700
James, you mentioned the phrase, murder your darlings.

625
00:27:14,720 --> 00:27:15,220
Mm-hmm.

626
00:27:15,220 --> 00:27:17,510
Just in case there's people listening who are

627
00:27:17,510 --> 00:27:19,800
not familiar with that because it sounds pretty morbid.

628
00:27:19,800 --> 00:27:24,170
But it's this idea that you may have these beloved passages or sentences or

629
00:27:24,170 --> 00:27:28,540
characters even, but you have to kill them for the greater good of the story.

630
00:27:28,820 --> 00:27:31,200
Does it get easier to murder your darlings?

631
00:27:31,540 --> 00:27:32,260
I think it does.

632
00:27:32,500 --> 00:27:38,540
I certainly have memories of being much younger and reacting badly to editors,

633
00:27:38,540 --> 00:27:44,580
possessively and somewhat neurotically, and storming around the house for a couple of days.

634
00:27:44,720 --> 00:27:48,050
And then I think just having enough of those experiences,

635
00:27:48,050 --> 00:27:51,380
you begin to realize that there is wisdom outside oneself.

636
00:27:52,240 --> 00:27:54,920
Editors have saved my ass so many times.

637
00:27:55,100 --> 00:27:58,380
I remember in a story for my story collection, my editor,

638
00:27:58,380 --> 00:28:01,660
Kate Napolitano, if you ever hear this, she was so brilliant.

639
00:28:01,660 --> 00:28:03,420
She had a great bullshit detector.

640
00:28:03,540 --> 00:28:05,660
I remember there was one line in one

641
00:28:05,660 --> 00:28:07,780
of my stories where I said, he wept.

642
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:09,370
And she put a little note

643
00:28:09,370 --> 00:28:10,740
there that said, who is he, Jesus?

644
00:28:11,320 --> 00:28:13,220
And I just cut it out.

645
00:28:13,280 --> 00:28:14,580
And I was like, oh, I don't know.

646
00:28:14,580 --> 00:28:15,989
Like, I think I like this,

647
00:28:15,989 --> 00:28:17,399
but she's saying cut it out.

648
00:28:17,419 --> 00:28:18,080
And I cut it out.

649
00:28:18,159 --> 00:28:20,260
And then I go back and revisit the story.

650
00:28:20,379 --> 00:28:22,399
Like, it just works so much better.

651
00:28:22,520 --> 00:28:24,359
Like, everything she's ever told me to

652
00:28:24,359 --> 00:28:26,199
cut out, I have done and followed.

653
00:28:26,360 --> 00:28:27,879
And it made it so much better.

654
00:28:27,879 --> 00:28:30,229
I totally agree that murdering all

655
00:28:30,229 --> 00:28:32,580
of that gets so much easier.

656
00:28:32,840 --> 00:28:36,080
It becomes a sociopath almost for your bad lines.

657
00:28:36,939 --> 00:28:38,740
I have a little counter to this.

658
00:28:39,020 --> 00:28:39,939
Gentle pushback.

659
00:28:40,399 --> 00:28:41,760
Obviously, yes, kill your darlings.

660
00:28:41,860 --> 00:28:42,639
Yes, of course, edit.

661
00:28:42,960 --> 00:28:43,980
And it does get easier.

662
00:28:43,980 --> 00:28:44,960
I think that's true.

663
00:28:45,420 --> 00:28:47,810
That said, sometimes things are difficult and people don't

664
00:28:47,810 --> 00:28:50,200
like them, but it doesn't mean you should cut them.

665
00:28:51,100 --> 00:28:54,140
I'm not saying you need to be willfully difficult, but like that

666
00:28:54,140 --> 00:28:57,180
you do things that even though they may not be immediately appealing.

667
00:28:57,460 --> 00:28:57,820
Right.

668
00:28:57,920 --> 00:28:59,320
It goes back to the TV conversation.

669
00:28:59,620 --> 00:29:02,600
So much TV is bad because it's like always rewarding.

670
00:29:02,600 --> 00:29:03,640
And there's a place for that.

671
00:29:03,780 --> 00:29:04,850
But there's also a place

672
00:29:04,850 --> 00:29:05,920
for things that don't reward immediately.

673
00:29:06,100 --> 00:29:08,260
I'm just saying that in the killing

674
00:29:08,260 --> 00:29:10,420
of your darlings discussion, you have to balance.

675
00:29:10,700 --> 00:29:11,300
Do I need this?

676
00:29:11,340 --> 00:29:11,940
Is this bad?

677
00:29:12,120 --> 00:29:12,740
Who is this telling?

678
00:29:12,740 --> 00:29:13,960
And that's why it makes it hard.

679
00:29:13,980 --> 00:29:14,220
Right.

680
00:29:14,220 --> 00:29:15,780
Maybe I haven't done a good enough job

681
00:29:15,780 --> 00:29:17,340
of writing the thing I want to write.

682
00:29:17,340 --> 00:29:18,380
It's not that I should cut it.

683
00:29:18,380 --> 00:29:19,380
It's that I should write it better.

684
00:29:19,660 --> 00:29:20,580
That's the challenge.

685
00:29:20,580 --> 00:29:22,080
That's what good editing is.

686
00:29:22,080 --> 00:29:24,170
It's sort of sliding those moments where there's

687
00:29:24,170 --> 00:29:26,260
no life or there's less life than you need.

688
00:29:26,260 --> 00:29:28,340
It's not telling you to cut something.

689
00:29:28,340 --> 00:29:30,180
It's telling you it needs to be better.

690
00:29:30,220 --> 00:29:31,500
Everyone needs an editor.

691
00:29:32,260 --> 00:29:37,720
One of my greatest writing teachers, Michelle Herman, she had this famous mark that she would put on your stories and

692
00:29:37,720 --> 00:29:43,180
they were brackets and she would bracket a sentence and she would just write out on the side, do better here.

693
00:29:43,180 --> 00:29:43,340
Mm-hmm.

694
00:29:43,340 --> 00:29:43,620
Yeah.

695
00:29:43,620 --> 00:29:43,660
Yeah.

696
00:29:43,660 --> 00:29:43,920
Yeah.

697
00:29:43,980 --> 00:29:45,580
And it really worked.

698
00:29:45,820 --> 00:29:49,460
I'm having flashbacks to my early writing career and how a comment

699
00:29:49,460 --> 00:29:53,100
like that would have caused me to just have a total breakdown.

700
00:29:53,100 --> 00:29:55,620
And I would take it so personally.

701
00:29:55,980 --> 00:29:58,680
But I do think with experience and perspective,

702
00:29:58,680 --> 00:30:01,380
you realize that there's nothing to take personally.

703
00:30:01,420 --> 00:30:03,180
We all benefit from having

704
00:30:03,180 --> 00:30:04,940
other people weigh in on stuff.

705
00:30:05,020 --> 00:30:06,380
We all make bad art on

706
00:30:06,380 --> 00:30:07,740
the way to making good art.

707
00:30:07,740 --> 00:30:10,420
And I think like, it's just the way it is.

708
00:30:10,780 --> 00:30:12,220
As we wrap up this episode,

709
00:30:12,220 --> 00:30:13,660
do you have any advice for others?

710
00:30:13,660 --> 00:30:14,860
For other writers out there?

711
00:30:15,220 --> 00:30:18,660
One of the things I found has been very useful in

712
00:30:18,660 --> 00:30:22,100
talking to my students, especially when we're working on short stories.

713
00:30:22,100 --> 00:30:25,220
But more than ever, when we're working on novels

714
00:30:25,220 --> 00:30:28,340
is thinking about your process and thinking about your schedule.

715
00:30:28,580 --> 00:30:30,780
I'm teaching a novel workshop next semester.

716
00:30:30,780 --> 00:30:34,620
And one of the first things that we're going to do is talk

717
00:30:34,620 --> 00:30:38,460
about our schedule, making time for the practice of writing in our daily lives.

718
00:30:38,460 --> 00:30:40,900
And I think that has always been something

719
00:30:40,900 --> 00:30:43,340
that has saved me, even when I'm feeling stuck.

720
00:30:43,340 --> 00:30:47,899
I know that every Monday from seven in the morning to 10,

721
00:30:47,899 --> 00:30:52,459
I'm going to spend three hours that Monday thinking about my work.

722
00:30:52,459 --> 00:30:53,779
Even if I don't put a mark

723
00:30:53,779 --> 00:30:55,100
on the page, I'm still thinking about it.

724
00:30:55,100 --> 00:30:56,179
I'm still wrestling with it.

725
00:30:56,179 --> 00:30:59,759
I think Peter Ho Davies, who teaches at University of Michigan, talks about you don't

726
00:30:59,759 --> 00:31:03,340
have to write every day, but you still need to touch your work every day.

727
00:31:03,820 --> 00:31:08,460
And I think that is something that has always helped me when I feel

728
00:31:08,460 --> 00:31:13,100
like I'm failing and I don't know my way out of a particular story.

729
00:31:13,100 --> 00:31:19,379
My instinct, going back to what Sam said about like how we deal with conflict, is to run away,

730
00:31:19,379 --> 00:31:25,659
is to procrastinate, is to not, it's just watch a really bad television show or do something that's not writing.

731
00:31:25,659 --> 00:31:27,559
But that's the time when I need to

732
00:31:27,559 --> 00:31:29,459
go to the work and sit with it.

733
00:31:29,459 --> 00:31:33,020
Writing is more a verb than it is a noun.

734
00:31:33,020 --> 00:31:34,860
It's the process.

735
00:31:34,860 --> 00:31:38,219
What you're doing is like focused on the process.

736
00:31:38,219 --> 00:31:39,580
That's the art.

737
00:31:39,580 --> 00:31:41,320
And if you happen to have something at the end

738
00:31:41,320 --> 00:31:43,060
of it that other people don't have, that's the art.

739
00:31:43,060 --> 00:31:44,020
That's the art that other people can read.

740
00:31:44,020 --> 00:31:44,780
That's great.

741
00:31:44,780 --> 00:31:46,700
That allows you to do it again.

742
00:31:46,700 --> 00:31:48,400
I don't know if anybody ever gave

743
00:31:48,400 --> 00:31:50,100
this advice, but it feels like someone did.

744
00:31:50,100 --> 00:31:51,899
So I'm just going to go with it.

745
00:31:51,899 --> 00:31:55,179
There really are no rules, right?

746
00:31:55,179 --> 00:31:59,199
If you look at the great works of literature, Tolstoy

747
00:31:59,199 --> 00:32:03,220
does everything that he wants to do because he can, right?

748
00:32:03,220 --> 00:32:06,360
So it's really only the rules inherent to

749
00:32:06,360 --> 00:32:09,500
the story at hand that you need to discover.

750
00:32:09,500 --> 00:32:11,260
And then everything else you

751
00:32:11,260 --> 00:32:13,020
just figure out as you're going.

752
00:32:13,060 --> 00:32:15,320
So do not be hidebound, do

753
00:32:15,320 --> 00:32:17,580
not be rule-bound, do not be afraid.

754
00:32:17,580 --> 00:32:19,560
Go courageously into the work

755
00:32:19,560 --> 00:32:21,540
and the work will reward you.

756
00:32:21,540 --> 00:32:22,340
Lovely.

757
00:32:22,340 --> 00:32:23,500
I love that.

758
00:32:23,500 --> 00:32:25,419
Thank you all for joining me today.

759
00:32:25,419 --> 00:32:26,060
Thanks, Sam.

760
00:32:26,060 --> 00:32:26,980
Thank you.

761
00:32:26,980 --> 00:32:28,220
Thanks, Sam.

762
00:32:30,659 --> 00:32:31,940
Thanks for listening.

763
00:32:31,940 --> 00:32:35,140
To see a transcript of this episode or to

764
00:32:35,140 --> 00:32:38,340
